      "math_academy_id": "q-110376",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-7.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(5)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-7)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks.",
